-- §10b Every `between` as a cross-document REFERENCE, beside whether it lands on a layer here.
#
# ONE ROW PER FILED REFERENCE, with the filing its notation resolves to, or the typed reason it
# resolves to nothing.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ REPORTED AND NOT ASSERTED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A part reference that dangles is
# a VIOLATION and `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc` says so, because a fusion sum cannot be computed
# without it. A `between` that dangles is ORDINARY, and the schema says so in the element's own
# refusals: *a group eliminating against a member that files nothing, or against a layer folded
# into a different composed layer, is ordinary, and a keyref forcing the subset would make the
# honest case unfileable*. Writing the part rule's shape here would accuse a filing the model
# names as correct.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THAT LICENCE IS WHY THIS RELATION DID NOT EXIST FOR SO LONG. This tree's pressure is
# *a rule reads only what it needs*, so the thing that normally forces a reference into being a
# named object is the check that reads it. `between` is the ONE cross-document reference no rule
# MAY check, so nothing pulled it into view, and it stayed a raw table read as EVIDENCE for a
# number rather than as a POINTER at another document.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHICH IS A GENERAL HOLE IN THE PRESSURE MODEL, not a fact about eliminations: anything the
# model licenses but no rule may judge is invisible to a tree that grows by rules. §7's
# observations are the answer, and this is one.
-- eliminations/references.sqlc against eliminations/resolved.sqlc, the whole beside the half.
SELECT a.composition                        AS "composition!",
       a.composed_layer                     AS "composed_layer!",
       a.quantity                           AS "quantity!",
       a.party                              AS "party!",
       a.layer                              AS "layer!",
       coalesce(r.resolved_filing, '(outside this corpus)') AS "lands_on!",
       (r.resolved_filing IS NOT NULL)      AS "resolves!"
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(eliminations/resolved.sqlc)
) r USING (composition, composed_layer, quantity, seq)
ORDER BY (r.resolved_filing IS NOT NULL), 1, 2, 3, a.seq
